content.text:
verdict: findings
draft_version_seen: 2
checks:
  - {claim: "Summary 1 / F1 (SFIA ARCH definition and levels)", question: "Does SFIA 9 ARCH say what is quoted for the skill and levels 5–6?", source_reopened: "https://sfia-online.org/en/sfia-9/skills/solution-architecture", result: pass, notes: "All quoted fragments present verbatim (level-6 'Leads the development of architectures for complex solutions', 'Manages trade-offs and balances...', 'across multiple projects or initiatives')."}
  - {claim: "F1 (Open CA)", question: "Does the Open CA page say 'overall technical vision for a particular solution'?", source_reopened: "https://www.opengroup.org/certifications/certified-architect-open-ca", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F1 / Summary 2 (IASA)", question: "Does IASA say the SA 'owns the value of the delivery...' and list technical design documentation and formal models?", source_reopened: "https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/job_description.html", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; deliverables found as 'technical design documentation' and 'formal models of the system'; 'solution designs/impact analyses' correctly left UNVERIFIED (page has 'impact on the overall topology' only)."}
  - {claim: "F1 / Summary 1 (TOGAF scope, secondhand)", question: "Do Berrisford and Wikipedia say 'single project or project release' and that TOGAF's skills framework does not recognize the role?", source_reopened: "http://grahamberrisford.com/... ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solution_architecture", result: pass, notes: "Berrisford verbatim; Wikipedia sense confirmed (fetch returned it paraphrased as 'does not formally recognize'); both correctly marked secondhand."}
  - {claim: "Summary 1 (Gartner 'a specific solution')", question: "Does Wikipedia carry the Gartner definition?", source_reopened: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solution_architecture", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim 'an architectural description of a specific solution'; Gartner itself not opened, stated in Limitations."}
  - {claim: "Summary 1 (vendor titles; Google 'Cloud Architect')", question: "Are the certification titles as stated?", source_reopened: "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-architect ; AWS SAA-C03 ; Microsoft Azure Solutions Architect Expert", result: pass, notes: "Google's exact title is 'Professional Cloud Architect'; AWS and Microsoft use 'Solutions Architect'."}
  - {claim: "F2 (Queensland 'Decides which technologies to use')", question: "Is the quoted text on the page verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://www.forgov.qld.gov.au/.../solutions-architect", result: fail, notes: "Page reads 'Solutions architects decide which technologies to use'; draft's quoted 'Decides which technologies to use' alters the wording — re-quote or mark paraphrase. 'the link between the needs of the organisation and the developers' and ARCH/CNSL/TECH/EMRG-5 verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F2 (AWS / Microsoft quotes)", question: "Do the vendor pages say what is quoted?", source_reopened: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/... ; https://learn.microsoft.com/...azure-solutions-architect/", result: pass, notes: "AWS verbatim (truncated before 'and future projected needs'); Microsoft both sentences verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F2 / Summary 3 (IASA EA tool selection; Hohpe bounds; Advice Process)", question: "Do the three sources say what is quoted?", source_reopened: "IASA job_description ; https://martinfowler.com/articles/architect-elevator.html ; https://martinfowler.com/articles/scaling-architecture-conversationally.html", result: pass, notes: "IASA sentence verbatim and in the Enterprise Architect description; Hohpe 'within agreed-upon bounds of general principles or a published IT strategy' verbatim; 'anyone can make an architectural decision' verbatim with the two-consult qualifier."}
  - {claim: "F2 / Summary 3 (reconciliation)", question: "Is the two-level reconciliation clearly labeled as the report's judgment?", source_reopened: "n/a", result: pass, notes: "Labeled medium and as judgment in F2, Summary 3 and Limitations."}
  - {claim: "F3 / Summary 4 (Avanscoperta)", question: "Do the DDD pages say 'Finding boundaries... can't be delegated' and the bounded-context sentences, and name the relationship kinds?", source_reopened: "https://www.avanscoperta.it/en/context-mapping/ ; https://www.avanscoperta.it/en/bounded-context/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; partnership, conformist, ACL, open host all named."}
  - {claim: "F3 / Summary 4 (SAFe abstracts + scrum-master.org)", question: "Do the SAFe abstracts and the secondary summary say what is quoted?", source_reopened: "https://framework.scaledagile.com/system-architect ; /solution-architect ; https://scrum-master.org/en/role-of-the-sae-or-system-architect-in-an-safe-train/", result: pass, notes: "SAFe abstracts verbatim incl. 'Solution Train'; 'Define the systems, subsystems and interfaces on which solutions are developed' and 'in line with ART's business vision' verbatim on scrum-master.org; enablers/NFR/capacity from the SAFe abstract."}
  - {claim: "F3 (team boundaries contested)", question: "Do Mind the Product and continuous-architecture.org say so?", source_reopened: "https://www.mindtheproduct.com/product-leaders-guide-to-team-topologies/ ; https://www.continuous-architecture.org/practices/team-topologies/", result: pass, notes: "'blocked by other teams' and product-leader responsibility verbatim; 'after the architecture is defined' verbatim, workshop includes PO/PMs and architects."}
  - {claim: "F4 / Summary 5 (Pettersson)", question: "Are the Pettersson quotations verbatim, and is the architect list 'influence' or 'decide'?", source_reopened: "https://pettersson.dev/governance/architect-productowner/", result: fail, notes: "'Architects optimize the system...' , both decision lists, and 'The architect provides direction... within those guardrails...' verbatim; but 'The confusion rarely comes from responsibilities. It comes from unclear decision rights.' could not be found verbatim (page says confusion stems from unclear decision rights; verbatim sentence found is 'When both roles understand their decision rights, conflict becomes collaboration') — mark paraphrase or re-quote. Also the source heads the architect list 'Architects Make Architectural Decisions', stronger than the draft's 'influence'."}
  - {claim: "F4 (ondata; SAFe Product Management)", question: "Do the sources say what is quoted?", source_reopened: "https://ondata.blog/... ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-management/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim, including 'works closely with the System Architect... as leaders of the Agile Release Train'."}
  - {claim: "F4 (Lawson failure mode)", question: "Does Lawson say 'setting yourself up for failure' and mention missed patterns / conflicting requirements?", source_reopened: "https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/smell-single-backlog-service-based-architecture-james-lawson", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; 'regularly missing architectural patterns and conflicting requirements' present."}
  - {claim: "F5 / Summary 6 (Fowler)", question: "Is the 'wish you could get right early' definition Johnson's and the 'inversely proportional' rule Fowler's?", source_reopened: "https://martinfowler.com/ieeeSoftware/whoNeedsArchitect.pdf (read as saved PDF)", result: pass, notes: "Both verbatim on p.3; Johnson attribution correct; Architectus Oryzus mentoring passage present."}
  - {claim: "F5 / Summary 6 (Thoughtworks Radar, ARB RACI)", question: "ADRs Adopt with source-control quote; Advice Process Trial with the ARB sentence; RACI as described?", source_reopened: "thoughtworks.com radar (both) ; https://theartofcto.com/frameworks/architecture-review-process", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; RACI rows match including CTO Accountable for approve/reject."}
  - {claim: "F5 / Summary 6 (TOGAF Phase G via Conexiam)", question: "Does Conexiam present the three non-compliance options as the architect's?", source_reopened: "https://conexiam.com/togaf-adm-phase-g-ensure-value-with-implementation-governance/", result: fail, notes: "Three options verbatim, but the page says 'the choice of action belongs to a Stakeholder', not the architect; draft's 'the architect's three options' (F5) misassigns the decision — correct to 'options the architect presents to the stakeholder'."}
  - {claim: "Alternatives ('typically covers all missions and functions')", question: "Which source, and does it describe the enterprise architect?", source_reopened: "http://grahamberrisford.com/...", result: unverified, notes: "Phrase is Berrisford's TOGAF definition of the 'Enterprise' level of description, not of the enterprise-architect role; no source named in the draft for it."}
  - {claim: "Summary 1 ('plain architect has no definition at all')", question: "Is this sourced?", source_reopened: "n/a", result: unverified, notes: "Negative claim from the sweep only; acceptable if reworded 'in no source opened'."}
confidence_labels: "F3 'high' is doubtful: its sources are Avanscoperta (self-labeled 'secondary (community primary)') and a SAFe abstract plus a secondary summary — no primary text in the scheme's sense; medium is the defensible label for the decomposition finding and Summary 4's first sentence. All other labels defensible; no confidence/likelihood phrase combined."
top_changes: ["F5 and Summary 6: the enforce/relief/change choice belongs to the stakeholder per Conexiam; the architect presents the options — reword.", "F4/Summary 5: mark 'The confusion rarely comes from responsibilities. It comes from unclear decision rights.' as paraphrase or replace with the verbatim sentence; render the architect list as decisions the architect makes, not 'influences'.", "F2: re-quote Queensland as 'Solutions architects decide which technologies to use' (or mark paraphrase).", "F3 and Summary 4 first sentence: downgrade high to medium (no primary source per the scheme).", "Alternatives: cite Berrisford for 'covers all missions and functions' and note it defines the Enterprise scope, not the EA role; reword Summary 1's 'no definition at all' to 'in no source opened'."]
